steps.body:
To pick among three or more values, put another ternary in the false branch: `a ? x : b ? y : z`. JavaScript checks the conditions top to bottom and the first true one wins, just like an else if chain.

Write one condition per line so it reads like a table:

```javascript
const speed = 70;
const label =
  speed > 100 ? "fast" :
  speed > 50 ? "steady" :
  "slow";
console.log(label); // steady
```

Order matters the same way it does with else if: put the most specific condition first. The last value has no condition and catches everything else.

A chained ternary only picks a value. When a branch has to *do* something, such as print, update a counter, or run several statements, use if/else instead:

```javascript harness
const speed = 120;
{{code}}
console.log(`warnings: ${warnings}`);
```

```javascript
let warnings = 0;
if (speed > 100) {
  console.log("Slow down");
  warnings += 1;
}
```

